This is the 3rd fact about operating systems. The first recognizable OS was the GM-NAA I/O, created in 1956 for the IBM 704. It was designed to automatically start the next job when the current one finished, which was the very first step toward automating the hardware we use in our implementation.

This is the 4th story about operating systems. In the early 1960s, "Time-Sharing" changed everything. Systems like CTSS allowed multiple users to log in simultaneously, creating the illusion that each person had the entire computer to themselves, a concept fundamental to the concurrency I am testing here.

This is the 5th story about operating systems. The Multics project was an ambitious attempt to create a "computer utility." While it was ultimately too complex for its time, its failure led two researchers, Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie, to start a smaller, simpler project that they jokingly called Unics.

This is the 6th fact about operating systems. Unix was originally written in assembly language, which meant it could only run on one specific type of machine. In 1973, the team took the radical step of rewriting the entire kernel in the C programming language to make it portable.

This is the 7th fact about operating systems. The invention of the "Pipe" in Unix allowed the output of one program to become the input of another. This modularity is a great parallel to how I handle data packets in our implementation, where one layer’s output feeds directly into the next.

This is the 8th story about operating systems. While Unix ruled the labs, the 70s saw the rise of CP/M for microcomputers. It introduced the concept of a Basic Input/Output System (BIOS), which separated the high-level OS commands from the specific, messy details of the hardware.

This is the 9th fact about operating systems. In 1980, Microsoft didn't actually write their first OS. They bought "QDOS" (Quick and Dirty Operating System) from Seattle Computer Products, renamed it MS-DOS, and licensed it to IBM, changing the course of computing history forever.

This is the 10th story about operating systems. The Graphical User Interface (GUI) wasn't invented by Apple or Microsoft, but at Xerox PARC. Their "Alto" computer used a mouse, windows, and icons back in 1973, though it was considered too expensive for the general public at the time.

This is the 11th fact about operating systems. Apple’s Lisa and the subsequent Macintosh brought the "Desktop Metaphor" to the masses in 1984. It forced the OS to handle complex memory management for graphics, much more demanding than the simple text-based shells of the past.

This is the 12th story about operating systems. Windows 1.0 was released in 1985, but it wasn't a true OS; it was just a "shell" that ran on top of MS-DOS. It struggled with "Cooperative Multitasking," where one frozen app could crash the entire computer.

This is the 13th fact about operating systems. The debate between "Monolithic" and "Microkernels" defined the late 80s. Monolithic kernels (like Linux) put everything in one big block, while Microkernels (like Mach) try to keep the core as small as possible for better stability.

