This is the 23rd fact about operating systems. The "Blue Screen of Death" became a cultural icon of the 90s. It was the OS’s way of saying it hit a "Kernel Panic"—a state where the system detects a fatal error it cannot safely recover from without rebooting.

This is the 24th story about operating systems. In 2008, Google released Android, which is essentially a specialized Linux kernel with a Java-based UI on top. It proved that a desktop-grade kernel could be stripped down to fit into the palm of our hands.

This is the 25th fact about operating systems. Virtualization allows one "Host" OS to run multiple "Guest" OSs simultaneously. This is done via a Hypervisor, which tricks each guest into thinking it has direct access to the physical CPU and memory.

This is the 26th fact about operating systems. Containerization, popularized by Docker, takes virtualization further. Instead of emulating a whole computer, it just "packages" the application and its dependencies, sharing the host’s kernel to save massive amounts of overhead.

This is the 27th story about operating systems. Real-Time Operating Systems (RTOS) are used in things like cars and medical devices. Unlike Windows, an RTOS guarantees that a specific task will be completed within a strict time limit, where a delay could be life-threatening.

This is the 28th fact about operating systems. Modern OSs use "Journaling" file systems. If the power cuts out, the journal keeps a log of what was being written, allowing the OS to recover our data without a long and painful disk check on the next boot.

